  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "A virus is a tiny infectious particle that is much smaller than most cells.
    Unlike living cells, a virus cannot grow, produce energy, or reproduce independently.
    It needs to enter a suitable host cell and use the cell\u2019s machinery to make
    new viruses.\nA virus contains genetic material, which can be either DNA or RNA.
    This genetic material carries the instructions needed to produce new virus particles.
    It is protected by a protein covering called the capsid. Many viruses also have
    surface proteins or spikes, which help them recognize and attach to specific
    host cells.\nThe infection process begins when a virus approaches a suitable
    host cell and attaches to its surface. After attachment, the virus enters the
    cell or delivers its genetic material inside. The viral DNA or RNA then uses
    the host cell\u2019s machinery to make copies of the viral genetic material and
    produce viral proteins and other components.\nThese newly produced components
    assemble together to form complete new virus particles. As the process continues,
    many new viruses can form inside the infected cell. The newly formed viruses
    are then released from the host cell. Once outside, they can reach other suitable
    cells and begin the infection process again.\nIn this way, a virus uses a host
    cell to reproduce, turning one invading virus into many new virus particles and
    allowing the infection to spread."
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: ecf77945acdc26144b2f70e021c9fd31, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 6468205213134445944, guid: ecf77945acdc26144b2f70e021c9fd31, type: 2}
